implementationPlan.description:
Test first, then implement terminal-decision guards (DoD 1–3). Load test-driven-development. Extend the existing proposal describe/fixtures in StateManager.test.ts with compact table-driven cases: (a) PENDING ADD_RAIL approval for GLOBAL, EPIC and TASK adds the exact rail once and one PROPOSAL_APPROVED entry; a second approval returns the stored object and does nothing; (b) first rejection of a PENDING proposal preserves rails and produces one PROPOSAL_REJECTED entry; repeated rejection returns that stored object unchanged; (c) both conflicting directions, APPROVED→reject and REJECTED→approve, across the three scopes refuse with the exact MoeError below and preserve everything; (d) repeated approval of MODIFY_RAIL and REMOVE_RAIL also no-ops, even though the original rail no longer exists; (e) both methods retain the existing 'Proposal not found: <id>' failure for unknown IDs. Before the retry/refusal capture the resolved record, relevant project/epic/task/proposal file bytes, all three in-memory rail lists and activity.log bytes after `await stateManager.flushActivityLog()`. Afterward flush again and assert byte-for-byte/state equality, no new emitted events, no writeEntity calls, and no appendActivity calls. Subscribe only for the replay/refusal and unsubscribe in finally. Ensure the first PENDING resolution really applied/rejected (not a vacuous all-no-op test), and compare resolvedAt/resolvedBy as part of the stored record. Example assertions:
```ts
const approved = await stateManager.approveProposal(proposal.id);
await stateManager.flushActivityLog();
const recordBytes = fs.readFileSync(path.join(moePath, 'proposals', `${proposal.id}.json`), 'utf8');
expect(await stateManager.approveProposal(proposal.id)).toBe(approved);
expect(fs.readFileSync(path.join(moePath, 'proposals', `${proposal.id}.json`), 'utf8')).toBe(recordBytes);
await expect(stateManager.rejectProposal(proposal.id)).rejects.toMatchObject({ code: -32003, codeName: 'NOT_ALLOWED', message: '[NOT_ALLOWED] resolve proposal not allowed: proposal already resolved' });
```
Add notAllowed to the existing ../util/errors.js import. Immediately after each missing-proposal check, before applyRailChange/writeEntity or timestamp creation, use:
```ts
// approveProposal
if (proposal.status === 'APPROVED') return proposal;
if (proposal.status !== 'PENDING') throw notAllowed('resolve proposal', 'proposal already resolved');
// rejectProposal
if (proposal.status === 'REJECTED') return proposal;
if (proposal.status !== 'PENDING') throw notAllowed('resolve proposal', 'proposal already resolved');
```
Preserve all PENDING logic and its error handling, awaited writes and event order; retain existing failed-rail-application and missing-original tests. Preserve the StateManager line budget by changing each existing missing-proposal block to the equivalent `if (!proposal) throw new Error(\`Proposal not found: ${proposalId}\`);`, offsetting the added guard lines; do not change stateModules.test.ts or restructure unrelated methods. Do not deduplicate rails by text: two different PENDING proposals remain independent.

Prove the existing /ws boundary enforces the resolution contract (DoD 2, plus concurrent replay regression). Add a nested rail-proposal resolution describe under Plugin endpoint (/ws), reusing connectAndCollect, the real StateManager fixture, and random loopback port. Seed a typed RailProposal via state.createProposal; consume STATE_SNAPSHOT before sending command frames. Table-test an already APPROVED row receiving REJECT_PROPOSAL and an already REJECTED row receiving APPROVE_PROPOSAL. Do not stub the resolution methods or synthesize an ERROR response. Example frame/assertion:
```ts
client.ws.send(JSON.stringify({ type: 'REJECT_PROPOSAL', payload: { proposalId: proposal.id } }));
expect(JSON.parse(await client.nextMessage())).toMatchObject({ type: 'ERROR', operation: 'REJECT_PROPOSAL', code: -32003, codeName: 'NOT_ALLOWED', message: '[NOT_ALLOWED] resolve proposal not allowed: proposal already resolved' });
```
Snapshot rail/proposal files, in-memory state and flushed activity.log before each refusal and assert unchanged afterward. Test repeated same decisions return PROPOSAL_UPDATED with the original resolved record but append no new activity or state events; this direct acknowledgment is allowed and is not a new resolution broadcast. Add a two-client concurrent APPROVE_PROPOSAL test against one PENDING EPIC ADD_RAIL: both receive the same resolved record, rail occurs once, and exactly one PROPOSAL_APPROVED activity exists. Add a competing approve/reject test: exactly one successful PROPOSAL_UPDATED and one fixed ERROR, one terminal audit event, and rails correspond to whichever decision won; do not assume scheduling order. Use the current fixture without enabling extra broadcast wiring, register waiters/buffer from connect time, and clean up sockets, subscriptions and activity writes in finally/teardown. Production WebSocketServer.ts already has the required mutex and error handling and must not be changed for this task.
